Prior notes on Creative Commons and software
=============================================

This project's code was previously licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. That choice
was reconsidered (2026-09-09) and the code moved to Apache-2.0 — kept
here because the reasoning is still relevant to why the docs/book/website
content (LICENSE-DOCS) remains under a Creative Commons licence while the
code does not:

Creative Commons licences were written for creative works rather than for
software, and Creative Commons themselves advise against using them for
code — no patent grant, no clear position on linking/derivative-work
boundaries for compiled software, and incompatibility with combining the
work with other Apache/MIT-licensed code. Those are real problems for a
language runtime meant to be embedded and redistributed, which is why the
engine now uses Apache-2.0 (explicit patent grant, well-understood
Source/Object-form and Derivative Work definitions, broad ecosystem
compatibility) while prose content — where those software-specific
problems don't apply — stays under CC BY-SA 4.0.

